  "notes": "Every figure on this row is Ubiquiti's, read 2026-11-21. https://dl.ui.com/ds/usw-pro-48-poe_ds.pdf (HTTP 200, application/pdf, 450,616 B, 2 pp) prints 'Networking interface (48) GbE RJ45 ports / (4) 1/10G SFP+ ports', 'PoE interface (40) PoE/PoE+ (Pins 1, 2+; 3, 6-) / (8) 60W PoE++; PoE/PoE+ ...', 'Total available PoE 600W', 'Max PoE wattage per port by PSE PoE+: 32W / PoE++: 64W'. https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/usw-pro-48-poe (HTTP 200, 656,334 B, rendered spec table) prints '1 GbE RJ45 48 (40 PoE+; 8 PoE++) (1G/100M/10M)', '10G SFP+ 4', 'Total PoE Availability 600W', 'PoE Ports PoE+ 40 / PoE++ 8', and 'PoE+ 32W / PoE++ 60W' - agreeing with the datasheet on PoE+ and 4 W lower on PoE++; the datasheet figure is used. The vendor prints counts, not positions: the 8 PoE++ sockets are modelled as Ports 41-48, the upper end of the 48-port bank, the same ordering used on the other Pro rows here, because leaving them undeclared made every socket report a 15.4 W 802.3af ceiling. Before this correction no copper port on the row declared poeOut, so engine/media.js's undeclared-port default gave every socket the 802.3af ceiling of 15.4 W and web/faceplate.js printed 'PoE out 15.4 W' on sockets the datasheet rates 802.3at/802.3bt. The SFP+ cages are not PSE ports and stay undeclared."
